## 3. Key Tasks and Technical Implementation

### A. OSPFv2 Area Segmentation

Static Router IDs were assigned, and interfaces were placed into their respective logical areas:

- **Area 0 (Backbone):** Inter-router point-to-point links (`10.1.12.0/30` and `10.1.23.0/30`).
- **Area 10 (Branch):** End-user LAN segment (`192.168.10.0/24`) attached to R1.
- **Area 20 (Data Center):** Server LAN segment (`192.168.20.0/24`) attached to R3.

### B. ABR (Area Border Router) Role Verification

R1 and R3 were configured as Area Border Routers (ABRs): each has interfaces in Area 0 and in one non-backbone area. They are responsible for converting Type 1 and Type 2 (intra-area) Link-State Advertisements into Type 3 (Summary LSAs) for propagation into the Backbone Area 0 and neighboring areas.

R2 has all of its interfaces in Area 0, so it operates as a backbone router (not an ABR).

### C. Interface Security & Route Summarization (`area range` and `passive-interface`)

- Access interfaces facing LAN segments (Gi0/0/1 on R1 and R3) were set as `passive-interface` to suppress unnecessary Hello packet transmission and minimize attack surface.
- Route summarization was configured at the ABR boundaries to reduce routing table overhead and isolate local link-state flapping from outer areas.

